Andy Johnson <usenet@take_this_out.swervy.tk> carefully considered, then mistyped:
> Roar Lauritzsen wrote:
>> Is there a DIFFERENCE (in REAL LIFE, I mean) between "wear" and "put 
>> on"? Can't I "put on" a cap, or "wear" a ring? Is there something (in 
>> NetHack, now) that can be both W)orn and P)ut on, with different 
>> effects? If no, THEN FOR TOOTH'S SAKE, MAKE "W" AND "P" SYNONYMOUS!!!
> The difference is that in real life when you're not WEARing anything, 
> you're naked. You can be naked and still have jewelry on.
> In nethack it makes sense because you only Wear armor. There are enough 
> situations I can think of where you might want to take off all of your 
> armor but who cares about rings/ammys/lenses, or take off all your 
> rings, for this to be useful in NetHack. There are certain things that 
> can happen to armor that makes it fundamentally different from other 
> things that might be worn.

This seems to be an arbitraty line drawn in the sand.  You could make the claim
that it would be helpful to remove all objects from your arms (weapon, 
sheild, rings, gloves) or from your torso (cloak, body armor, amulets), or
everything EXCEPT your arms (keep those gloves on).

Merging the two commands together has the property of obscuring to a degree 
what various objects affect (could this scroll of destroy armor get rid of this
amulet of restful sleep?).  It also frees up a letter key, which might allow
some new interesting action (wipe could be mapped to W, or we could use P 
to procrastinate).

